    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1257">

                <pb facs="1107" n="793" xml:id="pg793"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72/635</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Antwerp</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Diederich</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Consulate General</hi>, <lb/>
                    <placeName>
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Antwerp</hi></placeName>, <date
                        ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-document-content" calendar="gregorian"
                        notAfter="1914-08-26T19:20:00-05:00" notBefore="1914-08-26T00:00:00+00:00"
                            ><hi rendition="#italic">undated</hi></date>. <lb/> [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Received August 26, 1914, 7:20 p.m.</hi>] </dateline>
                <p rendition="#center">[Telegram]</p>
                <p>Your cables 22, 23, 25 August, welfare.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d1257-f1"/> All
                    communications Belgium save. Ghent, Bruges, and seacoast cut off. Gibson, after
                    two days’ sojourn here, has started back to Brussels this noon. Hope he will get
                    safely through firing lines. Antwerp very calm though almost completely
                    isolated. At outbreak of war excitement was at fever heat and though many German
                    women and children were made to endure cruel hardships by the perhaps too
                    precipitate execution of the expulsion order no bodily harm or injury was done
                    them. I know whereof I speak. In times like these stories of horrible atrocities
                    will crop out everywhere but let Americans keep cool and remember <hi
                        rendition="#italic">audiatur et altera pars</hi>.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Diederich</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
